  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Microglossum fumosum, commonly known as the smoky woodtuft, is a small ascomycete fungus belonging to the Leotiaceae family that produces distinctive dark, club-shaped fruiting bodies. This species is found in wooded areas throughout New England and feeds on decaying wood and leaf litter.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are characteristically dark brown to blackish, giving it the 'smoky' appearance reflected in its name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Club-shaped form",
      "description": "Distinctive club or spoon-shaped fruiting bodies (ascomata) that are typically 1-3 cm tall",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth fertile head",
      "description": "The upper fertile portion appears smooth and uniform, contrasting with a slightly paler stalk",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Microglossum fumosum thrives in moist, shaded forest floors and decaying wood substrates throughout New England's deciduous and mixed woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other members of the Leotiaceae family, Microglossum fumosum releases its microscopic spores from sac-like structures called asci, which can burst explosively when conditions are right to disperse spores efficiently through the forest air.",
